<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n2110" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ămārĭco</span></b> ,
			āvi, ātum, āre, v.
			a.
		amarus, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>to make bitter</i> (eccl. Lat.). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">(liber) faciet amaricari ventrem tuum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vulg.
					Apoc. 10, 9</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">10, 10</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, <i>to excite</i>, <i>to irritate</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ecce repulsi sunt, qui amaricant,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Aug. Enn. in Psa. 65</span></i>
			, n. 15.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
